Plan evaluator execution before Rust generation

Expose rule evaluation as RAM functions with explicit nested loops,
branches, calls, and scalar computations so optimization passes can
change execution without reconstructing Rust control flow.

Lower shared rule prefixes, adaptive join selection, proof pruning,
canonical binding checks, and insertion guards before code generation.
Keep the linear query-planning form out of the final function bodies.

Plan compact storage capabilities, invariant keys, index layouts, and
coordinate conversions independently of Rust names and data types.
Preserve eligibility diagnostics and the existing storage fallbacks.

Expand stratified model traversal into RAM preparations, graph and
owner loops, transport operations, history bindings, and change checks.
Plan indexed storage views and desugared schedule control flow before
selecting their Rust representations.

Preserve the equality barrier, deferred function phases, and read-borrow
lifetimes through explicit stratum and closure bodies in both backends.
Expose inherited candidate scoring and proof retention, model traversal,
and mapped-prefix walks as RAM operations instead of emitter decisions.

Plan total-member completion loops and relation weights before emission
so fallback materialization and representative choices remain visible
to RAM passes.

Retain evaluator behavior and runtime operations while exposing their
compiler decisions for independent review and execution-based checks.

Share projection descriptions, closure operations, input-consumption
rendering, and row bookkeeping so equivalent native and indexed paths
cannot drift independently. Borrow existing plans directly and remove
obsolete renderer wrappers, parameters, and commented-out fragments.

Validate dynamic edits through a shared read-only storage interface
instead of exporting and mutating a disposable model. Preserve checked
errors and pending raw-result selection before applying the typed edit.
